;; talk/institution--protocol.scm — objections to surfaces/institution.scm, lens: protocol.

(objection 'o-institution-protocol-1 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'd-institution-reach :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/watchtower/last-resort-appointment.ts"
    "The leaning tells the board to let a watching service answer disputes while the people are away, and the surface later shows each account as covered or not covered. The only way the code lets a watching service answer for the organisation is a narrow authorization that the organisation signs first, and that authorization is built by buildSingleSignerHanko: one key, weight 1, threshold 1. The surface's own rule forbids any one person reaching the agreed share alone. I found no path in core that builds this authorization for a board whose members must act together, so an institution built the way this surface insists may never be able to appoint a service that works. The same laptops-off weekend then covers receiving too, because every frame, including the one that acknowledges an incoming payment, is certified only when members holding the threshold have signed it (core/entity/consensus/commit/precheck.ts). The decision treats 'a watching service covers disputes' as the settled part and only receiving as open. For the finance lead the cost is a weekend on which a counterparty puts an older state on the chain, nobody can answer inside the window, and the screen had said covered."))

(objection 'o-institution-protocol-2 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'institution-holders :lens "protocol"
  :claim (canon 'recovery-coverage-visible
    "This screen splits money into secured and could-lose and answers 'how much is secured' with collateral-mine alone. The protocol makes that share safe only while the organisation holds the latest state both sides signed and answers inside the window; nobody else has to supply it (law keep-evidence). The surface lists the need to know whether a watching service accepted or failed its job (institution-needs-coverage), but no screen shows it: the holders screen, the counterparty screen and the first screen show no figure for it, and the protocol ground has no figure for it either. So a director reads a secured total for money that is lost if a copy failed or a key is gone, and the surface's own canon rule says that coverage must be visible. A secured figure with no sign of whether it is covered is a safety claim the protocol does not back at that moment."))

(objection 'o-institution-protocol-3 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'institution-default-board-margin :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/consensus/leader/index.ts"
    "The default proposes an agreed share that the board still reaches with any one person missing. The same threshold certifies every frame, and the code flags any threshold at or below two thirds of the total weight as low quorum safety (getEntityQuorumSafetyWarning). The person is described as sharing approval with two to four colleagues. With equal weights, a board of two can only satisfy 'one person missing' with a threshold of one, which the surface itself refuses as solo action. A board of three can only satisfy it with two of three, exactly the case the code flags. Only a board of four or more reaches three of four and clears the flag. The surface does not say this, so for the smaller boards its default is either impossible or sets the organisation at a quorum the protocol itself warns about, where any two colleagues, or any two stolen keys, can move everything. The cost is that the margin against a missing person is paid for with the agreement the surface exists to guarantee."))

(objection 'o-institution-protocol-4 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'd-institution-signer-change :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/auth/authorization.ts"
    "The leaning says that an organisation that must replace someone moves to a new setup, as if that were a small cost. A new setup is a new entity, and every account is a relationship with one named entity (law accounts-are-bilateral). Moving collateral out of the old accounts needs either the counterparty's signature on a settlement, or leaving alone, which takes the whole challenge window (law exit-timing) and anything owed beyond the secured share is lost or only recorded as a debt (law unsecured-no-seizure). Starting to leave is a collective action: only messages, proposals and votes are individual in the allowlist, so an old entity that lost more members than the margin allows cannot even begin to leave, and everything in its accounts stays where it is. The decision weighs this against shareholder machinery that is out of scope but never says that the move itself is available only while a quorum still exists, and that a lost key beyond the margin leaves the collateral stuck with no action anyone can take."))

(objection 'o-institution-protocol-5 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'institution-default-fee-cap :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/paybook/payment-admission.ts"
    "The default says that if fees rose before the vote, 'that payment comes back and needs a new run', and the pay flow ends with a payment-by-payment account of what arrived and what came back. A voted request runs its exact actions inside the frame that carries the deciding vote, and a payment over its cap is refused by the admission check (HTLC_PAYMENT_MAX_SENDER_DEBIT_EXCEEDED). In the frame code a refused action is turned into a malformed-input error and thrown out of the whole frame (core/entity/consensus/frame/application.ts), not skipped, so as I read it the frame holding the last yes is discarded and no payment in the run leaves. The request stays waiting, the proposer cannot start another, and nothing in the protocol withdraws it; enough no votes are needed to drop it. The cost to the finance lead is a run of forty suppliers stopped by one stale fee, with the author's slot blocked, and a screen that promised a per-payment outcome."))

(objection 'o-institution-protocol-6 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'institution-default-response-time :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/account/config/dispute-config.ts"
    "The default is labelled as risk on the counterparty, and the leave decision speaks of a wait that is 'already twenty-five hours by default'. The challenge window is the sum of both sides' response times (law exit-timing), so a response time that covers a weekend lengthens the wait for every dispute on the account, including one the organisation starts itself to get out of a silent counterparty. With a one-hour hub it is about three days, not one, and the silent-counterparty decision and its changes-if still use the twenty-five hour figure. The organisation's money stays in the dispute longer by the surface's own default, so the risk is the holder's as well, and the figure the board is told when it votes to leave is wrong by the number the surface chose."))
